  "prediction": "**Summary of the Report on Cobra Dane Radar System**\n\nThe Government Accountability Office (GAO) has released a report on the Cobra Dane radar system, which is used by the Department of Defense (DOD) for various missions, including ballistic missile defense, space surveillance, and intelligence-gathering. The report highlights the capabilities of the Cobra Dane system and its contributions to these missions.\n\n**Capabilities and Contributions**\n\nThe Cobra Dane system can track and classify missile threats, determine if a threat was intercepted successfully, and provide discrimination capabilities to identify warheads from decoys. The system also supports space surveillance and intelligence-gathering missions. The report notes that the Cobra Dane system's location at Shemya Island, Alaska, provides operational advantages, including the ability to track missile threats from North Korea earlier in their trajectories.\n\n**Future Developments and Challenges**\n\nThe report notes that the development of the Long-Range Discrimination Radar (LRDR) system at Clear Air Force Station, Alaska, may reduce the DOD's reliance on Cobra Dane for ballistic missile defense and space surveillance. The LRDR system is expected to provide additional tracking and discrimination capabilities, and its location will allow it to track missile threats later in their trajectories. However, the report notes that the LRDR system is not expected to replace Cobra Dane, but rather complement its capabilities.\n\n**Funding and Sustainment**\n\nThe report notes that the Air Force and MDA plan to share funding for the operation and maintenance of Cobra Dane, as well as for three modernization projects. The Air Force has identified that it no longer has vendors that manufacture some critical parts, and failure of any of the three systems could result in Cobra Dane not being available to meet mission requirements. The report notes that the Air Force and MDA are working to expedite the mission system replacement project, but face challenges in expediting the other two projects without compromising Cobra Dane's operational availability.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe report concludes that the Cobra Dane system remains a critical component of the DOD's ballistic missile defense and space surveillance capabilities. However, the development of the LRDR system and other radar investments may reduce the DOD's reliance on Cobra Dane in the future. The report recommends that the DOD continue to prioritize the sustainment and modernization of the Cobra Dane system to ensure its continued availability for these critical missions.",
